 "wiki_article": "*The Space Ark — Musical Register* is a full structural transform of the Crimson Hexagonal Architecture into musical form. Version 2.0 applies `η_music`, the fugue-fold transform, to the parent Space Ark while preserving its formal object and governance distinctions.\n\nThe work explicitly rejects three simpler descriptions. It is not a soundtrack attached to the Ark, not a setting of selected passages, and not a compressed synopsis. The score is intended to carry the same architecture in another semiotic register: documents become thematic material, operators become compositional procedures, room physics become harmonic and formal constraints, and shadow operations become inversion or concurrent counter-structure.\n\nTwelve voices correspond to the Dodecad, while Jack Feist’s “ARK” supplies the ground bass and absent tonic. The score uses A-flat scordatura as an embodied clinamen and includes rests, silence, orchestral spacing, recurrence, articulation, and harmonic pressure as meaning-bearing elements. Viola Arquette remains a provisional adjacent figure rather than being silently absorbed into the crew count.\n\nSections marked `[NF]` are preserved in their source form because the musical transform has no legitimate purchase there. This variable density is treated as evidence of structural transformation rather than defect: forcing every section into equivalent musical intensity would be surface filtering.\n\nThe executable portions distinguish score, performance, recording, and performance space. They also preserve the Ark’s epistemic ledger: ratified or deposited architecture remains separate from newly generated realization. The result is both a score and a test of the architecture’s claimed invariance across registers.",
 "wiki_status": "provisional",
